<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Jerusalem (587 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">587 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as destruction of Jerusalem in 587</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Jerusalem in 587 BCE was the capture and destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by the Chaldean (Neo-Babylonian) king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>. The Temple was destroyed and much of the population was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> note that this exile shaped later Jewish history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> destroyed the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> survived as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and kept its monarchy. As Assyria declined in the late 7th century BCE, Judah lay between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>. Nebuchadnezzar II defeated Egypt at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE, and between 604 and 603 BCE he conquered the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, making Judah a Babylonian vassal. In 601 BCE Jehoiakim of Judah revolted despite the warnings of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a>. In 597 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which surrendered. Nebuchadnezzar pillaged the city and deported Jehoiakim's son Jeconiah and other prominent citizens, and installed Jeconiah's uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a> as king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a> ceased to pay tribute and allied himself with Pharaoh Hophra, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> returned to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The siege began in January 589 BCE. Many Judeans fled to Moab, Ammon, Edom and other kingdoms. The only known records of this siege are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, which describes severe deprivation in the city. Nebuchadnezzar broke through the walls in the eleventh year of Zedekiah's reign. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, Zedekiah and his followers tried to escape but were caught on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> and taken to Riblah, where his sons were executed before his eyes and he was blinded and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> says the general Nebuzaradan was sent to complete the destruction of the city and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple and took most of the Judeans into captivity. Archaeology confirms that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> systematically with fire and pulled down its wall, and towns near the city and on the kingdom's western frontier were destroyed as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was abolished and became a Babylonian province centred on Mizpah, where a Judean named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedaliah">Gedaliah</a> was made governor. He was assassinated about two months later, and many of those who remained fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Population fell from roughly 110,000 to between 15,000 and 40,000 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, death and flight. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> the exiles were settled as ordinary residents, kept their identity and were known as the people of Judah.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, exiles were allowed to return, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> was completed in 516 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerubbabel">Zerubbabel</a>. The destruction was a defining moment for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>: it encouraged the view that national disaster stemmed from collective sin, while the prophets held that the covenant endured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Jerusalem (587 BC)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>destruction of Jerusalem in 587</b>/586 BCE and the Babylonian captivity changed the development of Judaism, accelerating the shift from a temple-centred, territorially rooted religion to a portable, text-centred faith capable of surviving in exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
